# A sharp phase transition in linear cross-entropy benchmarking

**arXiv:** 2305.04954 (v1, 8 May 2023)
**Authors:** Brayden Ware, Abhinav Deshpande, Dominik Hangleiter, Pradeep Niroula,
Bill Fefferman, Alexey V. Gorshkov, Michael J. Gullans

## Abstract (paraphrase for index; see paper PDF for verbatim)
The linear cross-entropy benchmark (XEB), chi = 2^N * sum_x p(x) q(x) - 1, is the
standard scoring rule for random-circuit-sampling quantum-advantage demonstrations.
Via a stat-mech mapping of the two-copy dynamics of noisy random circuits, the paper
proves that as the noise strength epsilon per qubit per layer increases, XEB tracks
the state-preparation fidelity F in a global-white-noise regime (epsilon*N < c) but
then undergoes a SHARP PHASE TRANSITION at a critical value (epsilon*N)_c that depends
on the two-qubit gate set. For Haar-random 2-qubit gates (all-to-all) the analytic
threshold is (epsilon*N)_c = ln(5/2) ~= 0.9163, above which the XEB decay rate per
layer saturates at Delta{ln chi} ~= -ln(5/2) ~= -0.92 while F keeps decaying faster.
The 1D brickwork architecture shows the same qualitative transition at a slightly
different numerical threshold.

## Key quantitative anchors
- Analytic critical point (Haar 2q, all-to-all): (epsilon*N)_c = ln(5/2) ~= 0.9163.
- Asymptotic above-transition XEB decay rate: Delta{ln chi} -> -ln(5/2) ~= -0.92 per layer.
- Low-noise regime: F ~ (1-epsilon)^(N*d) (global white noise).
- Paper numerics: Fig. 2 at N=40.

## Replication scope note
This QC-100 replication reproduced the qualitative headline (chi/F blowup as epsilon*N
crosses ~ln(5/2)) on 1D brickwork N<=10 as a finite-size proxy for the true
large-N sharp transition. C1 (low-noise tracking) and C2 (sharp divergence onset)
confirmed; C3 (sharp N>=30 transition) and C4 (asymptotic -0.92/layer saturation)
are out of reach on CPU statevector and only qualitatively approached. See
report/REPORT.md and report/failure_analysis.md for the honest scope discussion.
